ct like this during this coven  doing the pandemic to  kind of help keep people's minds off  things for a while and have something  interesting  um to do and to participate in a project  so going back to that article in the la  times we  there an online version of the story  came out in early january  and we ended up um with a lot of  requests at that point  and then the editors liked the story so  much that they asked that they could put  it in print and  so they did so we had another article  come out in print  this was in february of this year so  esperanza was getting really worried  especially after the online article  she she took the request and graphed it  so here's showing the graph on versus  months versus number of participants  and you can see before the article came  out we were around 150 participants  and then after the online article came  out we were up in the 600s  again esperanza was worried about the  print version coming out and i said oh  don't worry who reads print versions of  newspapers these days  all of our requests are going to come  from online well i was  wrong because we ended up getting a huge  spike in request after the paper  the print version came out we had to  shut down the request site  and our business office told me that the  phones ended up burning as they said  with people calling to get seeds  esperanza and i each got emails and i  even after that got  letters from people asking to be part of  the project so um really it was  overwhelming to see on how people wanted  to be involved and wanted to help out  with the project  so this just shows one container of  envelopes that we sent out seeds  and we ended up um with over 800  subscribers to our project blog so a  great way to be able to  educate people about grand cherry and  golden berry  we're using eye naturalists this year  to help gather all that information  we're focusing on the three-line potato  beetle but we've also asked people to  take pictures and include information of  other insects or just of their plants in  general  so the way this works is they can take a  picture it comes with an identifier this  shows a three-line potato beetle  we can look at the observations we can  look at the pictures and we can also  graph this in our data from this year  this is just some other data that  esperanza included  in the slide to show an example of how  we can graph all of that data  um so we were the project  naturalist ends on the end of this month  and then we'll go in and  take a look and graph all that data  so again our primary information we want  it was on insects but we also asked  people to gather information  on other characteristics as we did in  previous years  we included a blog series this year on  crop improvement  so each time that we have a posting  it's about a different aspect of crop  improvement so we started out with plant  breeding  we had one on mutation breeding and this  is here so this is a more recent one  that savannah  dale put together on genome sequencing  and how that's part of crop improvement  we'll eventually work our way through up  getting up to  gene editing as well  i really wanted to expand connections  with chefs this year but because of  covid  that made it really difficult almost  impossible  but i really wanted to show the public  what could be done with these so i  thought  oh i know zoe dubrow in  plant mythology and zoe is an amazing um  baker i i follow her on twitter and i  saw some of the um just gorgeous  desserts that she she would make in post  so i i asked zoe if she was interested  in um giving our ground cherries a try  we had a lot of ground cherries at that  time  earlier in the season and if she would  write a blog for us  about about what she created so you can  see  this here is a tart that she made with  ground cherry curd it's absolutely  beautiful  she made these are a french  little cake that she learned about when  she was doing some research in france  um and plants not in baking but she um  learned about these i think they're  called financiers i probably have  slaughtered that name  but she put the ground cherry in that  and and had the husk  up at the top here she also grant um  dried the ground cherries and made  scones and used that ground cherry curd  she posted this information on instagram  she got a lot of hits i followed some of  the comments people asking where they  could get ground cherry and goldenberry  and and if you're interested go to our  website  zoe's got links to her recipes  on that post and we also have recipes on  that site for some of the  dishes that were part of our harvest to  them  so to summarize this part of the project  and and where are we going um  you know this has been as i mentioned  earlier so much fun  and just i'm just so happy to see how  people really want to help us  um i want to i need to write proposals  get more funding  we're kind of on a pause right now  because agencies that i wanted to apply  for for funding  um for outreach type um  initiatives were not taking proposals  this year  i had funding for esperanza only until  june of this year she's moved on to  another position so i really need to get  some more funding to get somebody in to  help with this  in the future i want to expand our  number of farm to table and csas connect  more with local chefs and  on some other ideas that we might be  able to come up with so if anybody has  any ideas of where i could apply for  funding  for some outreach and this kind of  community science i'd be happy to hear  about it  so now i'm going to move on and just  give you a bit of information about some  of the um  lab work research that we're doing and  how this is being guided by our  community science um  information and also some of our own  priorities  so we're fast tracking this improvement  gene editing um  you know part of it is that to create  enhanced germplasm  that has variability that breeders could  tap into  for improvement programs but before we  do the gene editing the first place we  had to start  was in developing a transformation  method and we started with ground cherry  because ground cherries are diploid  goldenberry is a  tetraploid and we felt with editing  diploid would be a much easier one to  tackle first so we base this on our  tomato transformation method  so we put ground cherry seeds in culture  they germinate  a difference between this and our tomato  method is that for tomato the cotyledons  are the explant  that works the best but for grand cherry  we found hypocotyls actually regenerate  plants  um that cod leads do not so that's a big  difference between tomato and ground  cherry  this is just our regeneration scheme  this isn't transformation  the medium contains growth regulators  that induces callusing in shoots  we then get plants that root um we take  cuttings and they're put into a test  tube  and the fully rooted plants are then  evaluated for  genotype with their genotype to look for  edits  when we have those rooted plants and  they've been genotyped if they're what  we're looking for they get transferred  to soil  we cover them with these water bottles  i've had the bottoms cut off  help us gradually acclimate those plants  to the real world because they've been  in these very humid culture vessels  we um take the caps off after a few days  and then completely remove the bottle  after about a week and you can see how  those plants transition  really well it's basically it's almost  like it's a hundred percent going from  tissue culture to soil for us  this is the vector system we've been  using we started using this  with tomato it's got this cassette here  with the npt2  um gene that confers resistance to the  antibiotic anamycin  we include the cannabis in in our  culture medium to select for only those  cells that received this vector  we have the cas9 being driven by the 35s  constitutive promoter  and our guide rnas are driven by the  arabidops